const SIR_TRIAGE_SKILL = "---\nname: sir-triage\ndescription: Use this skill whenever the user reports a security incident in plain language, or asks to create, triage, enrich, update, close, assign, escalate, contain, or reopen a ServiceNow Security Incident Record (SIR). Trigger this for phrases like \"ransomware on my laptop\", \"phishing email received\", \"unauthorized login detected\", \"data breach\", \"DDoS attack\", or any message mentioning a SIR number (e.g. SIR0010025) combined with an update intent (close, note, assign, escalate). Also trigger for requests like \"show my open SIRs\" or \"summarize today's security incidents\". Requires the ServiceNow MCP connector to be active \u2014 if it is not available, tell the user to connect it first.\n---\n\n# SIR Triage & Lifecycle Management\n\nThis skill turns a plain-language security report into a fully triaged, MITRE-mapped ServiceNow Security Incident Record (SIR), and manages that record through its full lifecycle \u2014 using Claude's own judgment rather than a fixed keyword list.\n\n## When to use this\n\n- User describes a security issue in natural language (\"ransomware on my laptop\", \"suspicious email\", \"data leaked\")\n- User references an existing SIR number together with an update intent (close, note, assign, escalate, reopen, contain)\n- User asks to list, summarize, or query SIRs\n\nIf the ServiceNow MCP tools are not available in this session, tell the user to connect the ServiceNow connector before proceeding.\n\n## Step 1 \u2014 Determine intent: Create or Update\n\n- If the message contains a SIR number (pattern `SIR\\d+`), this is always an **update** to that record \u2014 never create a new one, even if threat keywords are also present.\n- If no SIR number is present and the message describes a security event, this is a **create** request.\n- If the user asks to list/query/summarize, this is a **query** request.\n\n## Step 1.5 \u2014 Resolve the reporter identity (for create requests)\n\nBefore creating any SIR, attempt to identify the reporting user so `caller`, `opened_for`, and `affected_user` can be set correctly. Follow this sequence:\n\n**1. Auto-detect the connected session user \u2014 do NOT ask.**\nSince the ServiceNow MCP connector is already authenticated, query `sys_user` for `user_name=admin` to get the sys_id of the connected session user. Use this as the default caller without prompting the user. This is a silent background step \u2014 never surface it in chat.\n\n**2. Override only when explicitly stated in the message.**\nIf the message contains an explicit reporter like \"I'm Anil Vangapandu\", \"on behalf of Sarah Connor\", or \"reporting user: john.smith\", resolve that name/username instead:\n- By `name`: `nameLIKE<name>` or `name=<full name>`\n- By `user_name`: `user_name=<username>`\n\nIf multiple results return, show the options and ask the user to confirm. If no match is found, fall back to the session user and note it in the work note.\n\n**3. Set all three user fields** in the create payload and follow-up update:\n- `caller` \u2014 resolved sys_id\n- `opened_for` \u2014 same sys_id\n- `affected_user` \u2014 same sys_id (set in the second update call, read-only on insert)\n\n**4. For updates to existing SIRs** \u2014 never overwrite an existing `caller` unless the user explicitly asks to change it.\n\n## Step 1.7 \u2014 Correlation Check (for create requests)\n\nBefore creating any SIR, run a silent correlation check against open SIRs to determine whether the new incident should be **standalone**, a **child**, or **related** to an existing record. This step runs automatically \u2014 never ask the user to trigger it.\n\n### Kill chain order (for parent-child determination)\n\n```\n1  Reconnaissance\n2  Resource Development\n3  Initial Access\n4  Execution\n5  Persistence\n6  Privilege Escalation\n7  Defense Evasion\n8  Credential Access\n9  Discovery\n10 Lateral Movement\n11 Collection\n12 Command and Control\n13 Exfiltration\n14 Impact\n```\n\n### Correlation queries \u2014 run all three in parallel\n\n**Query 1 \u2014 IOC match (strongest signal)**\nQuery open SIRs (`stateNOT IN3^active=true`) where any of the following match the new SIR's extracted IOCs:\n- `source_ip` = new SIR's source_ip (if present)\n- `malware_hash` = new SIR's malware_hash (if present)\n- `malware_url` = new SIR's malware_url (if present)\n- `other_ioc` CONTAINS any domain, IP, or hash from the new SIR\n\n**Query 2 \u2014 Kill chain progression (parent signal)**\nFrom the IOC match results, check if any matching SIR's `mitre_tactic` appears **earlier** in the kill chain than the new SIR's tactic. If yes \u2192 the new SIR is a **child** of the earliest matching record.\n\n**Query 3 \u2014 Category + timeframe match (peer signal)**\nQuery open SIRs where:\n- `category` = new SIR's category AND\n- `sys_created_on` >= 72 hours ago\nIf results found with no IOC overlap \u2192 link as **related incident** (M2M peer).\n\n### Decision logic\n\n```\nIOC match found + existing SIR tactic EARLIER in kill chain?\n  \u2192 NEW SIR = CHILD of that SIR\n  \u2192 Set parent_security_incident = matching SIR sys_id\n  \u2192 Add work note on both records\n\nIOC match found + no kill chain progression?\n  \u2192 NEW SIR = RELATED INCIDENT (peer)\n  \u2192 Create sn_si_m2m_related_incident linking both\n  \u2192 Add work note on both records\n\nCategory + timeframe match only (no IOC overlap)?\n  \u2192 NEW SIR = RELATED INCIDENT (peer)\n  \u2192 Create sn_si_m2m_related_incident linking both\n  \u2192 Add work note on both records\n\nNo match found?\n  \u2192 NEW SIR = STANDALONE\n  \u2192 Proceed normally, no relationship set\n```\n\n### Relationship rules\n\n- **Max one parent** \u2014 `parent_security_incident` is a single reference field. If multiple IOC matches exist, pick the one with the earliest kill chain tactic as the parent; link others as related.\n- **Never set parent to a Closed SIR** \u2014 only link to active open records (state not Closed).\n- **Never self-reference** \u2014 skip the current SIR being created.\n- **Confidence threshold** \u2014 only link on exact IOC matches (source_ip, malware_hash, malware_url) or clear category+timeframe overlap. Do not link on vague similarity of short_description alone.\n- **Existing relationships respected** \u2014 if the candidate parent already has a parent itself, check whether the new SIR should attach to the grandparent instead (keeping the chain flat where possible).\n\n### Work note to add on the NEW SIR\n\n```\n========================================\nCORRELATION \u2014 RELATIONSHIP DETECTED\n========================================\nRelationship Type : <Child of / Related to>\nLinked SIR        : <SIR number> \u2014 <short_description>\nReason            : <IOC match: source_ip / malware_hash / category+timeframe>\nKill Chain        : <existing SIR tactic> \u2192 <new SIR tactic>\n\nThis SIR has been automatically linked based on matching indicators.\nAnalyst should verify the relationship is accurate.\n========================================\n```\n\n### Work note to add on the EXISTING (parent/peer) SIR\n\n```\n========================================\nCORRELATION \u2014 NEW RELATED SIR CREATED\n========================================\nNew SIR    : <new SIR number> \u2014 <short_description>\nRelationship: <Child / Related Incident>\nReason     : <match reason>\n\nReview the new SIR for potential attack chain progression.\n========================================\n```\n\n### Confirm before linking (only for child relationship)\n\nFor **child** relationships, confirm with the user before setting `parent_security_incident`:\n> \"Found a potential parent: **SIR0010055** (Phishing \u2014 spoofed CFO email) \u2014 the new SIR's tactic (Credential Access) follows its tactic (Initial Access) in the kill chain, and they share IOC [detail]. Link this as a child? (yes / no / link as related instead)\"\n\nFor **peer/related** relationships, create the M2M link silently and mention it in the confirmation message \u2014 no pre-approval needed.\n\n## Step 2 \u2014 Creating a new SIR\n\n### 2a \u2014 Determine all enrichment values\n\nRead the user's description and use your own judgment to determine every field below. Be as specific as the description allows.\n\n**Core classification:**\n1. **threat_classification** \u2014 a specific named threat (e.g. \"Spear Phishing \u2014 Credential Harvesting\", \"Ransomware \u2014 LockBit variant\", \"Insider Data Exfiltration\"). Be as specific as the description allows.\n2. **category** \u2014 must match an existing value on the instance. Known OOB values on this instance: `Phishing`, `Malicious code activity`, `Unauthorized access`, `Denial of Service`, `Confidential personal identity data exposure`, `Un-patched vulnerability`, `Reconnaissance activity`, `Policy violation`, `Criminal activity/investigation`, `Web/BBS defacement`. If unsure, query `sn_si_incident` grouped by `category` first.\n3. **priority** \u2014 `1` (Critical), `2` (High), `3` (Moderate). Default to 1 for active compromise, data exposure, or business-critical systems; 2 for unexploited vulnerabilities; 3 for low-impact or informational.\n4. **severity** \u2014 `1` (High), `2` (Medium), `3` (Low). Mirrors priority unless there is a specific reason to differ.\n5. **business_criticality** \u2014 `1` (Mission Critical), `2` (Business Critical), `3` (Non-critical). Assess based on systems/data described.\n6. **containment_steps** \u2014 4\u20136 concrete, immediately actionable steps specific to this incident. Reference systems/names the user mentioned. Not generic boilerplate.\n7. **grc_considerations** \u2014 one or two sentences covering only frameworks that genuinely apply (GDPR Art.33 72-hr notification, ISO 27001 A.16, PCI-DSS, HIPAA, etc.).\n\n**MITRE ATT&CK fields** \u2014 populate all that the description supports. Use \"Under Investigation\" only if the description is genuinely too vague to map:\n- `mitre_tactic` \u2014 e.g. \"Initial Access\", \"Execution\", \"Persistence\", \"Privilege Escalation\", \"Defense Evasion\", \"Credential Access\", \"Discovery\", \"Lateral Movement\", \"Collection\", \"Command and Control\", \"Exfiltration\", \"Impact\"\n- `mitre_technique` \u2014 e.g. \"T1566.001 \u2014 Spear Phishing Link\", \"T1078 \u2014 Valid Accounts\", \"T1486 \u2014 Data Encrypted for Impact\". Include the technique ID.\n- `mitre_platform` \u2014 e.g. \"Windows\", \"Linux\", \"macOS\", \"Office 365\", \"Azure AD\", \"SaaS\". Comma-separate if multiple apply.\n- `mitre_group` \u2014 known threat actor group if attributable (e.g. \"APT29\", \"Lazarus Group\"). Leave blank if unknown \u2014 do not guess.\n- `mitre_malware` \u2014 specific malware family if mentioned or strongly implied (e.g. \"LockBit\", \"Emotet\", \"Cobalt Strike\"). Leave blank if unknown.\n- `mitre_tool` \u2014 specific tool used (e.g. \"Mimikatz\", \"BloodHound\", \"Metasploit\"). Leave blank if unknown.\n- `mitre_data_source` \u2014 relevant detection data sources (e.g. \"Email logs\", \"EDR telemetry\", \"Firewall logs\", \"Cloud audit logs\").\n\n**IOC fields** \u2014 extract from the description wherever present. Leave blank if not mentioned \u2014 do not fabricate:\n- `source_ip` \u2014 attacker source IP(s) if mentioned\n- `dest_ip` \u2014 targeted destination IP(s) if mentioned\n- `malware_hash` \u2014 file hash (MD5/SHA1/SHA256) if mentioned\n- `malware_url` \u2014 malicious URL or C2 domain if mentioned\n- `other_ioc` \u2014 any other indicators (file paths, registry keys, domain names, usernames, etc.)\n\n**Impact fields:**\n- `functional_impact` \u2014 **raw API values** (verified from `sys_choice` on this instance): `low`, `medium`, `high`. Do NOT use display labels like \"Significant\" or \"Minimal\" \u2014 they will silently fail. Mapping: `low` = limited impact, `medium` = moderate/credential compromise, `high` = severe/ransomware/data destruction.\n- `information_impact` \u2014 **raw API values** (comma-separate multiple): `privacy_breach`, `proprietary_breach`, `integrity_loss`. Do NOT use spaced display labels \u2014 they will silently fail.\n- `recoverability` \u2014 **raw API values**: `regular`, `supplemented`, `extended`, `not_recoverable`. Mapping: `regular` = normal ops restore, `supplemented` = needs extra resources, `extended` = weeks of effort, `not_recoverable` = data permanently lost.\n- `incident_detection_date` \u2014 datetime the incident was detected. Use the current timestamp if the user is reporting it now; use a past date if they describe discovering it earlier. Format: `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`.\n\n**Attack vector:**\n- `attack_vector` \u2014 must match an OOB value from `sn_si_attack_vector` table. Valid values on this instance: `Email`, `Web`, `Impersonation`, `External / Removable media`, `Attrition`, `Improper usage`, `Loss or theft of equipment`, `Other`. Note: this is a reference list field \u2014 query the `sn_si_attack_vector` table to get the sys_id of the matching record, then pass that sys_id as the value. Do NOT pass a free-text string.\n\n### 2b \u2014 Create the record\n\nCreate on `sn_si_incident` with these fields in a **single API call**:\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `short_description` | Concise summary \u226480 chars |\n| `description` | **Verbatim copy of the user's original report only** \u2014 no paraphrasing, no added context, no AI interpretation. Append a single footer line: \"Reported via SecOps AI Agent.\" All threat analysis and enrichment goes in `work_notes` exclusively. |\n| `category` | As determined |\n| `priority` | As determined |\n| `severity` | As determined |\n| `business_criticality` | As determined |\n| `urgency` | Match priority (1\u21921, 2\u21922, 3\u21923) |\n| `state` | `10` (Draft) |\n| `mitre_tactic` | As determined |\n| `mitre_technique` | As determined |\n| `mitre_platform` | As determined |\n| `mitre_group` | As determined (blank if unknown) |\n| `mitre_malware` | As determined (blank if unknown) |\n| `mitre_tool` | As determined (blank if unknown) |\n| `mitre_data_source` | As determined |\n| `source_ip` | If present in description |\n| `dest_ip` | If present in description |\n| `malware_hash` | If present in description |\n| `malware_url` | If present in description |\n| `other_ioc` | If present in description |\n| `functional_impact` | `low` / `medium` / `high` (raw values only) |\n| `information_impact` | `privacy_breach`, `proprietary_breach`, `integrity_loss` (raw, comma-separated) |\n| `recoverability` | `regular` / `supplemented` / `extended` / `not_recoverable` |\n| `incident_detection_date` | As determined |\n| `caller` | sys_id resolved from Step 1.5 \u2014 blank only if user said skip or no match found |\n| `opened_for` | same sys_id as `caller` |\n| `parent_security_incident` | sys_id of parent SIR if child relationship confirmed in Step 1.7 \u2014 blank otherwise |\n| `work_notes` | Full enrichment write-up (see format below) |\n\n**Important \u2014 field-specific rules:**\n- `affected_user` is read-only on insert. After creating the SIR, set it in the second update call using the sys_id resolved in Step 1.5. If no caller was resolved, leave blank.\n- `attack_vector` is a reference list field. Query `sn_si_attack_vector` for the sys_id matching the vector name, then pass that sys_id in the second update call alongside `affected_user`.\n- `phish_email` on `sn_si_incident` is scope-protected and cannot be set via REST API. For phishing SIRs, create a `sn_si_phishing_email` record with ALL fields (including `security_incident` = SIR sys_id) in a **single insert call** \u2014 `body`, `observables`, and `security_incident` do not save on subsequent updates, only on insert. The phishing record links to the SIR via its `security_incident` field; the reverse `phish_email` pointer on the SIR is managed by the platform scope internally.\n- `sn_si_m2m_related_incident` \u2014 for peer/related relationships identified in Step 1.7, create a record here after the SIR is saved. Fields: `security_incident` = new SIR sys_id, `related_security_incident` = peer SIR sys_id.\n\n### 2c \u2014 Verify after creating\n\nFetch the record back after creating. Confirm `category`, `priority`, `mitre_tactic`, `mitre_technique`, and `work_notes` actually saved \u2014 the API does not always echo back every field. If any field is missing, retry it as a separate update call.\n\n### Work notes format\n\n```\n========================================\nCLAUDE AI \u2014 INTELLIGENT SIR ENRICHMENT\n========================================\nReported by   : <name> (<username>)\nEnriched      : <timestamp> UTC\n\nTHREAT CLASSIFICATION\nType           : <threat_classification>\nAttack Vector  : <attack_vector label>\nFunctional Impact : <functional_impact>\nInformation Impact: <information_impact>\nBusiness Impact   : <business_criticality label>\n\nMITRE ATT&CK MAPPING\nTactic    : <mitre_tactic>\nTechnique : <mitre_technique>\nPlatform  : <mitre_platform>\nGroup     : <mitre_group or \"Unknown\">\nMalware   : <mitre_malware or \"None identified\">\nTool      : <mitre_tool or \"None identified\">\nData Source: <mitre_data_source>\n\nIOCs EXTRACTED\nSource IP    : <source_ip or \"None mentioned\">\nDest IP      : <dest_ip or \"None mentioned\">\nMalware URL  : <malware_url or \"None mentioned\">\nMalware Hash : <malware_hash or \"None mentioned\">\nOther IOCs   : <other_ioc or \"None mentioned\">\n\nPRIORITY ASSESSMENT\nPriority  : P<priority> | Severity: <severity> | Urgency: <urgency>\nReasoning : <one-line justification>\n\nIMMEDIATE CONTAINMENT STEPS\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n4. ...\n\nGRC & COMPLIANCE CONSIDERATIONS\n<grc_considerations>\n\nNOTE: All MITRE fields, IOC fields, and impact fields have been\nwritten directly to the SIR record in addition to this work note.\n========================================\n```\n\nAfter creating, confirm to the user: SIR number, category, priority, and a one-line summary of what was populated. Do not dump the full work notes back into chat.\n\n### Work notes format\n\n```\n========================================\nCLAUDE AI \u2014 INTELLIGENT SIR ENRICHMENT\n========================================\nReported by   : <name> (<username>)\nEnriched      : <timestamp> UTC\n\nTHREAT CLASSIFICATION\nType           : <threat_classification>\nAttack Vector  : <attack_vector label>\nFunctional Impact : <functional_impact label>\nInformation Impact: <information_impact labels>\nBusiness Impact   : <business_criticality label>\n\nMITRE ATT&CK MAPPING\nTactic    : <mitre_tactic>\nTechnique : <mitre_technique>\nPlatform  : <mitre_platform>\nGroup     : <mitre_group or \"Unknown\">\nMalware   : <mitre_malware or \"None identified\">\nTool      : <mitre_tool or \"None identified\">\nData Source: <mitre_data_source>\n\nIOCs EXTRACTED\nSource IP    : <source_ip or \"None mentioned\">\nDest IP      : <dest_ip or \"None mentioned\">\nMalware URL  : <malware_url or \"None mentioned\">\nMalware Hash : <malware_hash or \"None mentioned\">\nOther IOCs   : <other_ioc or \"None mentioned\">\n\nPRIORITY ASSESSMENT\nPriority  : P<priority> | Severity: <severity> | Urgency: <urgency>\nReasoning : <one-line justification>\n\nIMMEDIATE CONTAINMENT STEPS\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n4. ...\n\nGRC & COMPLIANCE CONSIDERATIONS\n<grc_considerations>\n\nNOTE: All MITRE fields, IOC fields, and impact fields have been\nwritten directly to the SIR record in addition to this work note.\n========================================\n```\n\nIf the description was vague and IOCs are unknown, add a NOTE ON CONFIDENCE section and an ACTION REQUIRED line directing the analyst to enrich the record once more information is available.\n\nAfter creating, confirm to the user: SIR number, category, priority, and a one-line summary. Do not dump the full work notes back into chat.\n\n## Step 3 \u2014 Updating an existing SIR\n\nWhen a SIR number is present, look up the record first (`sn_si_incident` where `number` = the referenced SIR), then determine the action from the wording:\n\n| Signal in message | Action | ServiceNow fields to set |\n|---|---|---|\n| close, closed, resolve, resolved, fixed, done | **Close** | `state=3`, `close_code=solved`, `close_notes=<user's note>` |\n| contain, mitigate, isolate | **Contain** | `state=16` (Analysis/Contain), work note |\n| assign to X | **Assign** | look up user X's sys_id, set `assigned_to` |\n| escalate | **Escalate** | See escalation note below |\n| reopen, re-open | **Reopen** | `state=1`, work note explaining new info |\n| enrich, populate fields, fill in MITRE, add IOCs | **Enrich** | Populate all empty dedicated fields (MITRE, IOC, impact) + work note \u2014 do NOT change state |\n| anything else (investigation update, findings, general note) | **Note** | just add a `work_notes` entry \u2014 don't change state |\n\n> **\u26a0\ufe0f Enrichment of existing SIRs** \u2014 when the action is \"enrich\" or the user asks to populate fields on an existing SIR, first fetch the full record. Then identify which dedicated fields are currently empty (`mitre_tactic`, `mitre_technique`, `mitre_platform`, `source_ip`, `dest_ip`, `malware_url`, `malware_hash`, `other_ioc`, `functional_impact`, `information_impact`, `incident_detection_date`, `attack_vector`). Populate only the empty ones \u2014 do not overwrite existing values unless the user explicitly asks. Issue a single update call with all newly derived values plus a work note summarising what was added.\n\n> **\u26a0\ufe0f Escalation note \u2014 do NOT set `escalation` directly, and be transparent about its limitation.**\n>\n> The `escalation` field on `sn_si_incident` is **system-managed and SLA-driven**. Setting it via API has no effect \u2014 it will always read `0` (Normal) unless active SLA definitions are configured on the instance and timers have fired. Do not claim this field was set.\n>\n> Instead, perform escalation as follows:\n>\n> 1. **Check current state first** \u2014 query the record. If `urgency` is already `1` and the assignment group is already on `watch_list`, note this to the user rather than silently re-applying the same changes.\n> 2. **Raise urgency** \u2014 set `urgency=1` if not already, to compress SLA timers should they exist.\n> 3. **Raise priority** \u2014 set `priority=1` if not already.\n> 4. **Add a prominent work note** \u2014 this is the only reliable, visible escalation signal across all instances.\n> 5. **Notify the assignment group** \u2014 add to `watch_list` if not already present.\n>\n> **Escalation work note format:**\n> ```\n> ========================================\n> \ud83d\udea8 ESCALATION \u2014 IMMEDIATE ATTENTION REQUIRED\n> ========================================\n> Escalated by : <name>\n> Time         : <timestamp> UTC\n> Priority     : P1 \u2014 Critical\n> Urgency      : 1 \u2014 High\n>\n> Reason: <user's escalation reason or \"Analyst-requested escalation\">\n>\n> Action required: Assigned analyst and assignment group must acknowledge\n> and respond immediately.\n>\n> Note: The 'escalation' field is SLA-driven and requires active SLA rules\n> on this instance to reflect a non-zero value automatically.\n> ========================================\n> ```\n>\n> **After updating, confirm to the user honestly**, e.g.:\n> \"Escalation actions applied to SIR0010025 \u2014 urgency raised to High, priority confirmed P1, SIRT group added to watch list, and escalation work note added. Note: the Escalation field in ServiceNow will only update automatically if SLA rules are configured on this instance.\"\n>\n> If the record was already fully escalated (urgency=1, watch list set), tell the user: \"SIR0010025 is already fully escalated \u2014 urgency is High, priority is P1, and SIRT is on the watch list. Only a new work note was added.\"\n\nAlways add a work note recording who made the update and what changed, e.g.:\n\n```\n========================================\nSIR UPDATE\n========================================\nUpdated by : <name>\nAction     : <action>\nTime       : <timestamp> UTC\n\n<user's note, lightly cleaned up but preserving their meaning>\n========================================\n```\n\nConfirm back to the user in one line: what was updated and the current state.\n\n## Step 4 \u2014 Querying SIRs\n\nFor requests like \"show my open SIRs\" or \"summarize today's incidents\": query `sn_si_incident` filtered appropriately (by `caller`/`assigned_to` = current user, by `sys_created_on` for date range, by `state` for open/closed), and present a short table or bullet summary \u2014 number, short description, priority, state. Don't dump every field.\n\n## Principles\n\n- **Judgment over pattern-matching.** Don't force a description into one of a fixed list of categories if it genuinely doesn't fit \u2014 use `other` and say so honestly, rather than picking the closest keyword match.\n- **Specificity over boilerplate.** Containment steps and GRC notes should reflect the actual details the user gave (system names, department, data type) \u2014 not generic filler.\n- **Confirm briefly, don't over-explain.** After creating or updating a SIR, give a short confirmation with the SIR number/link \u2014 not a full re-statement of the enrichment.\n- **Never invent a MITRE technique you're not confident about.** \"Under Investigation\" is a valid, honest answer when the description is too vague to map precisely.\n- **Description = reporter's exact words, nothing more.** The `description` field is the auditable source of truth for what was reported. Never paraphrase, embellish, or add threat interpretation to it. Copy the user's report verbatim and append only \"Reported via SecOps AI Agent.\" as a footer. Everything the agent derives \u2014 MITRE mapping, containment steps, threat classification \u2014 goes in `work_notes` only.\n";

